ontrol cards (♥2, BJ, RJ) and a safe pair of 10s. The 7-card straight will break up a few of my structures and create a few more singles, but the advantage of clearing seven cards at once outweighs this. I am going to play my longest possible straight: 8-9-10-J-Q-K-A.\n\n**Evaluation:** Playing the 7-card straight (8-9-10-J-Q-K-A) is the best move because it clears a significant portion of my hand, leaving me with a manageable structure to win the game.\n\n**Reason:** I possess a Rocket and a high 2, providing me excellent control. While my hand contains some disjointed cards, the 7-card straight is the most efficient play to reduce my hand count, use isolated cards, and improve my position. The opponents don\'t have many bombs out, so I can take advantage of that and get some cards off the table. The 7-card straight maximizes this while preserving my high cards for later control if a bomb does come out.\n\n**Answer:** {"cards": ["♠8", "♠9", "♣10", "♥J", "♦Q", "♦K", "♣A"]}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand contains a powerful Rocket (Black Joker + Red Joker) and a high single (♥2), guaranteeing control. However, the rest of the hand consists of several disconnected singles and a triplet of 10s that conflicts with potential straights. The most efficient move is to play the long straight (8-9-10-J-Q-K-A), which consumes 7 cards at once, including several isolated singles (8, Q, K, A) that would otherwise be difficult to play.\n\nReason: Leading with the single 4 is risky because there are two 2s remaining in opponents\' hands; if an opponent plays a 2, I would be forced to break the Rocket or lose the lead. By playing the straight, I clear almost half my hand, forcing opponents to use a bomb to stop me. If they pass, I retain my strong control cards (Rocket, 2) and a pair of 10s to manage the few remaining cards (4, 9, J). This maximizes the utility of my cards and maintains tempo.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠8", "♠9", "♣10", "♥J", "♦Q", "♦K", "♣A"]}'}
