ate those single card plays. And, given that my 2 is the highest card I can play, it will guarantee a win, barring a bomb of course!\n\nWhat about an intimidation play? No. I need to get rid of my trash!\n\nSo, the play is clear. Pass. And hope to survive the onslaught.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold a hand of disjointed single cards: 4, 9, J, 2, and the Rocket (Black Joker, Red Joker). The opponent has played a very high pair (Aces). While I can beat this with my Rocket, doing so would be strategically fatal. I have three low cards (4, 9, J) that I need to dispose of. To do this successfully, I need to regain the lead three times. I possess exactly three cards capable of guaranteeing the lead in single-card play: the 2, the Black Joker, and the Red Joker. If I play the Rocket now, I combine two of those entries into one, leaving me with only two entries (the Rocket and the 2) to cover three losing cards. I would inevitably be left with a losing card at the end. By passing, I preserve the option to break the Rocket later and use the Jokers as individual high cards to win tricks against singles, which is my only realistic path to victory.\n\nReason: My hand requires 3 entries to clear 3 low singles (4, 9, J). I have exactly 3 high singles (2, Black Joker, Red Joker). Using the Rocket now reduces my entries to 2, making it impossible to clear my hand. I must pass and hope for a single-card game.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
